---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="About" description="Learn about Haxbyte and Doug Rosenberg">
	<div class="about-container">
		<h1>About Haxbyte</h1>

		<section class="about-section">
			<h2>Who I Am</h2>
			<p>
				I'm Doug Rosenberg. I spent 3+ years building enterprise systems—ERPs for global nonprofits,
				platforms for Fortune 10 companies, complex full-stack applications for manufacturers. I led
				architecture sessions, designed scalable databases, and shipped systems that handled thousands
				of concurrent users.
			</p>
			<p>
				Then I built two Squarespace sites for small businesses. And I realized something: most small
				business owners don't need enterprise complexity, but they also shouldn't settle for generic,
				slow, or fragile sites. They deserve the same engineering discipline and attention to performance
				that enterprises get—just without the overhead.
			</p>
			<p>
				Haxbyte is where I write about bringing that discipline to smaller, faster-moving projects —
				clean code, fast performance, architecture that doesn't turn into technical debt.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="about-section">
			<h2>What I Build</h2>
			<p>
				Fast, clean sites and small tools, built with the same rigor I applied to enterprise systems.
				The kinds of projects I take on and write about:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Small business sites</strong> — occasional referral projects, from Squarespace builds to fully custom sites</li>
				<li><strong>Redesigns</strong> — turning a dated or template-bound site into something that performs</li>
				<li><strong>Performance work</strong> — chasing sub-2-second loads, here and elsewhere</li>
				<li><strong>Accessibility</strong> — WCAG 2.1 AA as a baseline, not an afterthought</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="about-section">
			<h2>The Haxbyte Approach</h2>
			<p>
				Enterprise discipline, applied to smaller, faster-moving projects.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Clean, readable code</strong> — Doesn't become technical debt. The next person touching it (often future me) can understand and maintain it.</li>
				<li><strong>Mobile-first design</strong> — Built for how people actually use the web, not retrofitted after the fact.</li>
				<li><strong>Performance obsession</strong> — Sub-2-second loads. No bloat. No unnecessary frameworks.</li>
				<li><strong>Accessibility built in</strong> — WCAG 2.1 AA baseline, on every device.</li>
				<li><strong>Architecture that scales</strong> — Grows with the project instead of forcing a rewrite.</li>
				<li><strong>Security from the ground up</strong> — HTTPS, secure by default, no shortcuts or workarounds.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				No templates. No AI-generated design shortcuts. No vendor lock-in. Just intentional work built to last.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="about-section cta-section">
			<h2>Let's Talk</h2>
			<p>
				Want to talk shop, compare notes on a build, or just say hi? I'm easy to find.
			</p>
			<a href="/contact" class="cta-button">Get in touch</a>
		</section>
	</div>
</Base>

<style>
	.about-container {
		max-width: var(--max-width-prose);
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: var(--space-3xl) var(--space-md);
	}

	.about-container h1 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	.about-section {
		margin-bottom: var(--space-3xl);
		padding-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(11, 18, 32, 0.1);
	}

	.about-section:last-child {
		border-bottom: none;
	}

	.about-section h2 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	.about-section ul {
		list-style: none;
		padding-left: 0;
	}

	.about-section li {
		position: relative;
		padding-left: var(--space-lg);
		margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
	}

	.about-section li::before {
		content: '→';
		position: absolute;
		left: 0;
		color: var(--spark);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.cta-section {
		text-align: center;
	}

	.cta-button {
		display: inline-block;
		background-color: var(--link-orange);
		color: var(--white);
		padding: var(--space-md) var(--space-lg);
		border-radius: var(--radius-md);
		text-decoration: none;
		font-weight: 600;
		transition: opacity var(--duration-normal);
	}

	.cta-button:hover {
		opacity: 0.9;
	}
</style>
